#use(std/code)
#use(std/pipe-table)

#section{Pipe tables}::

#code{#use(std/pipe-table)} provides #code{#ptable} — a source-beautiful
plain-text table with a custom body reader: one row per line, cells split on
#code{|}, first row is the header. It is allowed to be pretty dumb, on
purpose; for markup in cells or anything structural, use #code{std/table}.

#code(lang: "plain")
	#ptable
		Name  | Role  | Since
		Kiril | infra | 2019
		Ann   | web   | 2021

Live:

#ptable
	Name  | Role  | Since
	Kiril | infra | 2019
	Ann   | web   | 2021

Markdown-style outer pipes are tolerated:

#code(lang: "plain")
	#ptable
		| Name  | Role  |
		| Kiril | infra |

Live:

#ptable
	| Name  | Role  |
	| Kiril | infra |

Corner cases:

#list
	#item: The body is a custom (non-markup) reader: cell text is plain — #code{#emph{…}} in a cell would be literal text, not markup. That dumbness is the plugin's contract.
	#item: Cell padding spaces are trimmed; alignment in the source is purely cosmetic.
	#item: There is no escape for a literal #code{|} in a cell — #code{|} always splits.
	#item: The first row is always the header; a ragged row (wrong column count) and an empty body are explicit errors.
